Let $I(t)$ be the solution to the equation $R \dot{I}+\frac{1}{C} I=0$ with initial condition $I(0)=I_0$. We have
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} \dot{I} &= -\frac{1}{RC} I \\ \frac{dI}{I} &= -\frac{1}{RC} dt \\ \int_{I_0}^I \frac{dI}{I} &= -\frac{1}{RC} \int_0^t dt \\ \ln I - \ln I_0 &= -\frac{t}{RC} \\ \ln \left(\frac{I}{I_0}\right) &= -\frac{t}{RC} \\ \frac{I}{I_0} &= e^{-t/RC} \\ I &= I_0 e^{-t/RC} \end{align*}
Thus, $c = I_0$.

Final answer: The final answer is I_0. I hope it is correct.

